courses=[]
def course(id,short,title,code,pages,desc,formula,refs,note=''):
 c=dict(id=id,short=short,title=title,code=code,pages=pages,description=desc,formula=formula,refs=refs,note=note,items=[]); courses.append(c);return c
def add(c,ref,title,body,final):
 c['items'].append(dict(ref=ref,title=title,body=body.strip(),final=final))
ml=course('ml','ML','Machine Learning','CSE 475',[3,4],'From learning fundamentals to worked decision trees, neural networks and Naive Bayes.', ['Standardization: z = (x - mean) / standard deviation. Fit statistics on training data only.','Gini(D) = 1 - sum(p_k squared). Split impurity = sum((n_j/n) Gini(D_j)).','Perceptron: z = w.x + b; step(z) = 1 for z >= 0, otherwise 0.','Precision = TP/(TP+FP); recall = TP/(TP+FN); accuracy = (TP+TN)/N.','F1 = 2TP/(2TP+FP+FN). Naive Bayes score(c) = P(c) product P(x_j|c).','Iterations/epoch = ceiling(N/batch size), when retaining the last partial batch.'],['Christopher M. Bishop, Pattern Recognition and Machine Learning.','Aurélien Géron, Hands-On Machine Learning with Scikit-Learn, Keras, and TensorFlow.','scikit-learn User Guide: https://scikit-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html'])
add(ml,'1(a)','What is reinforcement learning?', 'An agent learns by interacting with an environment. At each step it observes a state, chooses an action, receives a reward and moves to a new state. It learns a policy that maximizes expected cumulative reward, balancing exploration with exploitation. Example: a robot learns a route by receiving rewards for reaching its destination and penalties for collisions.','Learning actions through interaction and reward.')
add(ml,'1(b)','What does k represent in k-NN?','k is the number of nearest training samples consulted for a prediction. For classification, their labels vote; for regression, their values are averaged, possibly with distance weighting. Small k can be noisy; large k can blur local patterns. Choose k using validation.','k = number of neighbors used to predict.')
add(ml,'1(c)','Linear regression cost function and why','The usual cost is mean squared error: MSE = (1/n) sum(y_i - predicted_y_i)^2. Squaring prevents positive and negative residuals from cancelling and penalizes large errors. For ordinary linear regression, the objective is convex in the coefficients, which supports straightforward optimization. It also corresponds to maximum likelihood under independent Gaussian errors with constant variance.','Usually MSE; it is smooth and convex for ordinary linear regression.')
add(ml,'1(d)','Curse of dimensionality','As feature dimension grows, the space expands rapidly, so a fixed dataset becomes sparse. Distances can become less informative and models may need much more data to generalize. Feature selection, regularization and dimensionality reduction can help.','More dimensions make data sparse and learning harder.')
add(ml,'1(e)','Limitations of an MLP','A multilayer perceptron may need considerable labeled data, computation and hyperparameter tuning. It can overfit and is difficult to interpret. A plain fully connected MLP does not explicitly exploit spatial or temporal structure as CNNs or sequence models do. The question asks for a limitation; any clearly justified one is sufficient.','MLPs can overfit, require tuning, and lack built-in spatial/temporal structure.')
add(ml,'1(f)','Traditional programming versus machine learning','Traditional programming combines explicit rules with input data to produce outputs. Supervised machine learning uses example inputs and target outputs to learn a model, then applies that model to new inputs. Example: hand-written spam rules versus a classifier trained on labeled emails.','Explicit rules are written in programming; useful patterns are learned in ML.')
add(ml,'1(g)','Main limitation of ensemble learning','Combining several models usually increases training cost, memory use and prediction latency, and often reduces interpretability. Improvement also depends on useful diversity: highly correlated weak models may add little benefit.','Higher computational cost and lower interpretability are common trade-offs.')
add(ml,'2(a)','Sigmoid, ReLU and preference for ReLU','Sigmoid(z) = 1/(1+exp(-z)), with output between 0 and 1. ReLU(z) = max(0,z). Sigmoid saturates for large positive or negative inputs, so its derivative approaches zero. For positive inputs, ReLU has derivative 1, helping gradients propagate and making computation simple. ReLU can still produce dead neurons for persistently negative inputs. Sigmoid remains appropriate for binary classification output probabilities.','ReLU is usually preferred in hidden layers; sigmoid is useful at binary output.')
add(ml,'2(b)','How L1 regularization selects features','Minimize prediction loss + lambda sum|w_j|. The absolute-value penalty favors sparse solutions, pushing some coefficients exactly to zero. Features whose coefficients become zero are effectively excluded. Increasing lambda generally increases shrinkage; choose it with validation and scale features when coefficient magnitudes must be comparable.','L1 makes some weights zero, creating a sparse feature set.')
add(ml,'2(c)','Cross-validation process and purpose','Purpose: estimate generalization and compare models without repeatedly tuning on the final test set. Divide the training portion into k folds. Train on k-1 folds and validate on the remaining fold, repeat k times, then average the scores. Select hyperparameters and refit on the full training portion. Evaluate once on an untouched test set. Fit preprocessing separately within each training fold to prevent leakage. Diagram: folds 1..k rotate through validation while the other folds train. For time series, use time-respecting splits.','Rotate validation folds, average performance, and keep the final test set untouched.')
add(ml,'2(d)','TF-IDF and word relevance','Term frequency measures how often a term occurs in a document, often normalized. Inverse document frequency downweights terms appearing in many documents: a simple form is idf(t)=log(N/df(t)); practical variants use smoothing. TF-IDF = tf(t,d) x idf(t). A frequent word in one document that is rare across the corpus receives a large weight. A ubiquitous word receives a small weight. Example: in a corpus of 100 documents, a term in only 2 documents has idf=log(50).','High local frequency plus low corpus frequency gives high TF-IDF.')
add(ml,'2(e)','Outliers and RANSAC','Outliers can distort a fitted line, inflate squared-error loss and shift summary statistics. RANSAC repeatedly samples a minimal subset, fits a candidate model, measures which observations lie within a residual threshold, and retains a model with a strong inlier consensus. It then refits using inliers. Set a suitable threshold and sufficient iterations. It works best when a meaningful inlier model exists.','RANSAC fits a model supported by inliers rather than letting outliers dominate.')
add(ml,'2(f)','Feature scaling and standardization','Distance-based models can let a numerically large feature dominate; gradient optimization can also be poorly conditioned. Standardization uses z=(x-mean)/standard deviation. Example: mean height 160 cm, standard deviation 10 cm, height 175 cm gives z=(175-160)/10=1.5. Calculate mean and standard deviation from training data, then reuse them for validation/test. Trees generally do not require scaling for their split decisions.','Standardization expresses features in standard-deviation units.')
add(ml,'3(a)(i)','Parametric versus non-parametric models','A parametric model has a fixed finite parameterization chosen before observing the dataset size. Linear regression with a fixed number of features is an example. A non-parametric model has flexibility that can grow with the data, such as k-NN or an unconstrained decision tree. Non-parametric does not mean that no parameters exist. Parametric models may be simpler and data-efficient; non-parametric models can capture more complex patterns but may overfit.','Linear regression is parametric; k-NN and flexible trees are non-parametric.')
add(ml,'3(a)(ii)','Explain the sentiment-classification pipeline','Preprocessing: collect student comments about the government decision, assign sentiment labels, remove duplicates, split data, and turn text into features such as TF-IDF. Learning: train a classifier on training features and labels. Evaluation: predict held-out comments and compare with their true labels using suitable metrics. Prediction: apply the same fitted preprocessing and final model to new comments. In the scanned diagram, the labels in evaluation are the ground truth for comparison; do not feed test labels into training.','Labeled comments -> preprocessing/split -> training -> held-out evaluation -> new-comment prediction.')
add(ml,'3(b)(i)','Gini impurity: Weather versus Temperature', '''There are 8 examples: 4 Yes and 4 No. Parent Gini = 1-(4/8)^2-(4/8)^2 = 0.5.
Weather: Sunny has 3 No, so Gini=0. Overcast has 2 Yes, so Gini=0. Rain has 2 Yes and 1 No, so Gini=1-(2/3)^2-(1/3)^2=4/9.
Weighted Weather impurity = (3/8)0+(2/8)0+(3/8)(4/9)=1/6, about 0.1667. Gain=0.5-1/6=1/3.
Temperature: Hot has 1 Yes and 2 No, Gini=4/9. Mild has 1 Yes and 1 No, Gini=1/2. Cool has 2 Yes and 1 No, Gini=4/9.
Weighted Temperature impurity=(3/8)(4/9)+(2/8)(1/2)+(3/8)(4/9)=11/24, about 0.4583. Gain=1/24.
Using the multiway categorical split requested by this table, choose Weather because it leaves less impurity. A binary-split implementation must evaluate category partitions separately.''','Choose Weather: impurity 1/6 versus 11/24.')
add(ml,'3(b)(ii)','How boosting works','Boosting builds an ensemble sequentially. Each new learner focuses on what the current ensemble handles poorly. AdaBoost increases the influence of misclassified samples; gradient boosting fits the negative gradient of the current loss, often described as fitting residual errors. Combine learners by weighted voting or addition. Learning rate and stopping control overfitting.','Sequential learners correct earlier errors and combine into a stronger predictor.')
add(ml,'3(c)(i)','Steps for training a neural network','Prepare and split the data; scale inputs where appropriate. Choose architecture, activations, loss and optimizer. Initialize weights. For each mini-batch, perform a forward pass, compute loss, backpropagate gradients and update parameters. Repeat across epochs, monitor validation loss, tune with validation data and optionally use early stopping. Finally evaluate once on held-out test data.','Forward pass -> loss -> backpropagation -> update; repeat and validate.')
add(ml,'3(c)(ii)','Keras code and replacing sigmoid with softmax','Sequential stacks layers in order. Dense(16, activation="relu") creates a fully connected hidden layer with 16 units; the next Dense(16, ...) creates another. Dense(1, activation="sigmoid") creates one output between 0 and 1, suitable for binary classification with an appropriate loss. Its trainable parameter count depends on the unspecified input dimension d: (d+1)16 + (16+1)16 + (16+1)1 = 16d+305. Replacing sigmoid with softmax while retaining one output makes softmax always equal exp(z)/exp(z)=1, so it cannot distinguish classes. For mutually exclusive multiclass classification use multiple softmax outputs and a corresponding categorical loss.','A one-unit softmax always returns 1; retain sigmoid for a single binary output.')
add(ml,'4(a)','Vanishing gradient','During backpropagation, many small derivatives are multiplied through layers. The resulting gradients in early layers can become tiny, so those weights learn slowly. Saturating activations worsen this. Appropriate initialization, non-saturating activations, normalization and residual connections can help.','Early layers learn slowly when backpropagated gradients shrink toward zero.')
add(ml,'4(b)','Transfer learning','Reuse knowledge from a model trained on a source task for a related target task. For example, use a pretrained image encoder, train a new classifier head and optionally fine-tune selected layers. It can reduce target-data and training requirements; unrelated source data can cause negative transfer.','Adapt a pretrained model to a new task.')
add(ml,'4(c)','Why accuracy is insufficient for imbalanced data','If 99 of 100 cases are negative, a model that always predicts negative has 99% accuracy while detecting no positives. Inspect the confusion matrix, precision, recall, F1 and appropriate ranking metrics according to the cost of errors.','High accuracy can hide failure on the minority class.')
add(ml,'4(d)','Why differentiable activations?','Gradient-based learning uses derivatives through the chain rule to determine how changing weights changes loss. Activations should therefore be differentiable almost everywhere or have usable subgradients. ReLU is not differentiable at zero, but implementations choose a subgradient there, so it remains trainable.','Backpropagation needs derivatives or usable subgradients.')
add(ml,'4(e)','Metric for continuous-valued predictions','Use a regression metric such as MAE, MSE or RMSE. MAE averages absolute errors and is in the target unit. RMSE is also in the target unit and emphasizes large errors. Select according to the intended error cost.','MAE, MSE or RMSE are suitable regression metrics.')
add(ml,'4(f)','What are outliers?','Observations unusually far from the typical pattern. They may reflect measurement errors, rare valid events or a different data-generating process. Investigate before deleting them; automatic removal can discard important cases.','Outliers are atypical observations, not necessarily errors.')
add(ml,'4(g)','Hyperparameters and examples','Hyperparameters are settings chosen outside the main parameter-fitting process. Examples: k in k-NN, tree maximum depth, neural-network learning rate, regularization strength and batch size. Tune them on validation data rather than the final test set.','Hyperparameters control the learning setup; weights are learned parameters.')
add(ml,'5(a)','Perceptron, neuron analogy and numerical output','A biological neuron integrates signals and may fire; a perceptron computes a weighted sum and applies an activation. Inputs resemble incoming signals, weights resemble synaptic strengths, and the bias shifts the firing threshold. Given x=[1,-2], w=[0.5,0.25], b=1: z=(1)(0.5)+(-2)(0.25)+1 = 0.5-0.5+1=1. With step(z)=1 for z>=0, output=1. The analogy is simplified; a perceptron is not a complete biological model.','Net input 1; step-activation output 1.')
add(ml,'5(b)','Epoch, batch and iterations','An epoch is one complete pass through the training set. A batch is a subset used for one update. Divide 60,000 by 128: 468 full batches cover 59,904 examples, leaving 96. Keeping that final partial batch gives 469 iterations per epoch. If an implementation explicitly drops the partial batch, it performs 468 and omits those examples in that pass.','469 iterations when the final partial batch is retained.')
add(ml,'5(c)','Slope, derivative and gradient','Slope of a straight line is rise/run, Delta y/Delta x. A derivative is the limiting local slope of a one-variable function: f\'(x)=lim[h->0](f(x+h)-f(x))/h. For f(x)=x^2, f\'(2)=4, the slope of its tangent at x=2. A gradient collects partial derivatives for a multivariable function. For f(x,y)=x^2+y^2, gradient=(2x,2y); at (1,2) it is (2,4). Draw a line with a rise/run triangle, a curve with its tangent, and contour circles with an outward gradient arrow. The site supplies an explanatory figure below this answer.','Slope: line steepness; derivative: local slope; gradient: vector of partial derivatives.')
add(ml,'5(d)','k-means limitations and k-means++','k-means requires k in advance, is sensitive to initialization and outliers, converges to local optima and favors roughly compact, similarly scaled clusters under Euclidean distance. k-means++ chooses the first center randomly, then chooses later centers with probability proportional to squared distance from the nearest selected center. This spreads initial centers and often improves convergence and solution quality. It does not solve the choice of k, non-spherical clusters or outlier sensitivity.','k-means++ improves initialization, not every limitation of k-means.')
add(ml,'5(e)','Why k-NN is lazy; handling ties','k-NN stores the training examples and defers most modeling work until a query arrives, hence lazy learning. Resolve voting ties using a declared rule: distance-weighted voting, the class of the nearest tied neighbor, or a deterministic class order. Odd k reduces binary-class vote ties but does not eliminate multiclass ties or equal-distance ambiguities.','It postpones work until prediction; use an explicit tie-breaking rule.')
add(ml,'5(f)','Feature importance and how to measure it','Feature importance describes how strongly a feature contributes to predictions or predictive performance. Methods include permutation importance on held-out data, standardized coefficients for suitable linear models, and tree impurity reductions. Permuting a useful feature generally reduces performance. Correlated features can share or hide importance; impurity-based importance can favor high-cardinality features. Importance is not proof of causation.','Measure contribution with held-out permutation tests or model-specific methods.')
add(ml,'6(a)(i)','What a confusion matrix represents','It cross-tabulates actual and predicted class labels. For binary classification it contains TP (positive correctly predicted), FN (positive missed), FP (negative incorrectly predicted positive) and TN (negative correctly predicted). State the row/column convention because libraries differ.','A confusion matrix counts each type of correct and incorrect prediction.')
add(ml,'6(a)(ii)','Accuracy, precision, recall and F1 calculation','Actual positives=100; actual negatives=50. TP=90, FN=100-90=10, TN=30, FP=50-30=20. Total=150. Accuracy=(90+30)/150=0.80=80%. Precision=90/(90+20)=9/11, about 81.82%. Recall=90/(90+10)=0.90=90%. F1=2(90)/(180+20+10)=180/210=6/7, about 85.71%. F1 balances precision and recall without the often-dominant TN count, making it informative for a positive minority class, although it ignores TN and is not universally the best metric.','Accuracy 80%; precision 81.82%; recall 90%; F1 85.71% (rounded).')
add(ml,'6(b)(i)','SVM kernel trick','A nonlinear feature map phi(x) may make data linearly separable in a higher-dimensional space. A kernel computes K(x,z)=phi(x).phi(z) without explicitly constructing that map. The SVM can optimize its margin using kernel values. Examples include polynomial and radial-basis-function kernels. Tune kernel parameters and regularization on validation data; a higher-dimensional representation does not guarantee good generalization.','Kernels compute feature-space inner products implicitly.')
add(ml,'6(b)(ii)','Identify the three fits and reduce overfitting','Reading the scanned figures from left to right: the smooth curved separator is the intended good fit, the straight separator is underfitting, and the highly wiggly separator is overfitting. Underfitting misses real structure; overfitting follows individual points or noise and generalizes poorly. Reduce overfitting with regularization, a simpler model, more representative data, early stopping, pruning or suitable augmentation. Compare training and validation performance to substantiate the visual diagnosis.','Left: good fit. Middle: underfit. Right: overfit, as intended by the sketch.')
add(ml,'6(c)(i)','Activity data: prior probabilities','Count all 10 rows in the original table. Play occurs in rows 1,3,4,6,9: 5 times. Study occurs in rows 2,7,10: 3 times. Sleep occurs in rows 5,8: 2 times. Therefore P(Play)=5/10=0.5; P(Study)=3/10=0.3; P(Sleep)=2/10=0.2.','Play 0.5; Study 0.3; Sleep 0.2.')
add(ml,'6(c)(ii)','Naive Bayes: Near, Bad weather, Off mood','Use score(c)=P(c)P(Near|c)P(Bad|c)P(Off|c), assuming conditional independence. For Play: 0.5 x (2/5) x (0/5) x (5/5)=0. For Study: 0.3 x (1/3) x (3/3) x (1/3)=1/30. For Sleep: 0.2 x (1/2) x (2/2) x (1/2)=1/20. Normalize: total=1/12, so posterior Play=0, Study=0.4, Sleep=0.6. Predict Sleep. With add-one smoothing across 3 deadline, 2 weather and 2 mood categories, scores are 9/392, 4/125 and 3/100 respectively; Study then narrowly wins. The answer therefore depends on the declared smoothing convention; the unsmoothed classroom calculation gives Sleep. Smoothing avoids a zero likelihood for an unseen feature/class combination.','Predict Sleep; unsmoothed posterior is 0.60.')
sec=course('security','Security','Computer Security & Forensics','CSE 461',[1,2],'Cryptography, secure systems and worked problems, with assumptions made explicit.',['Case-sensitive alphanumeric strings of length L: 62^L (repetition allowed).','Euler phi(n): count integers 1..n coprime to n. phi(14)=6.','ElGamal: y=g^x mod p; c1=g^k mod p; c2=M y^k mod p.','ElGamal decryption: M=c2 (c1^x)^(-1) mod p.','RSA textbook identity: (m^d)^e = m mod n. Real signatures use secure encoding such as RSA-PSS.','Toy rolling hash in this paper: d_i=(26 d_(i-1)+letter_value) mod 107.'],['William Stallings, Cryptography and Network Security: Principles and Practice.','NIST SP 800-38A: https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/38/a/final','NIST SP 800-63B: https://pages.nist.gov/800-63-4/sp800-63b.html'])
add(sec,'1(a)','Why the CIA triad matters','Confidentiality prevents unauthorized disclosure; integrity prevents or detects unauthorized modification; availability keeps authorized services accessible. A vulnerable environment can threaten all three: stolen grades violate confidentiality, altered grades violate integrity, and a disabled registration portal violates availability. Security controls must address the three goals together.','Protect secrecy, correctness and access to services.')
add(sec,'1(b)','Number of 6-character alphanumeric passwords','Assume 26 uppercase letters, 26 lowercase letters and 10 digits, with repetition allowed. Each of 6 positions has 62 choices, so total=62^6=56,800,235,584. If the examiner means case-insensitive letters, the answer is 36^6 instead. State the character-set assumption.','62^6 = 56,800,235,584 under the case-sensitive assumption.')
add(sec,'1(c)','Ciphertext shorter than the unicity distance','Unicity distance is an approximate ciphertext length beyond which language redundancy may leave roughly one plausible key for an idealized cipher model. Below it, several keys and meaningful plaintexts may remain consistent with the ciphertext. Even exhaustive search may not uniquely identify the intended plaintext without extra information. This is not a universal security guarantee for every cipher.','The attacker may face multiple plausible keys and plaintexts.')
add(sec,'1(d)','Most time-efficient access-control mechanism','The wording does not specify the query or data structure, so there is no universal winner. An access-control matrix directly represents subject-object permissions and is often the intended conceptual answer for arbitrary access queries. A densely indexed matrix can answer a specific permission lookup quickly but uses substantial space. ACLs organize permissions by object; capability lists organize them by subject. Explain that efficiency depends on the query and representation rather than claiming one always wins.','Likely intended: access-control matrix; actual efficiency depends on query and storage.')
add(sec,'1(e)','Two tools of integrity','A message authentication code detects unauthorized changes when a secret key is shared. A digital signature also detects changes and can authenticate the signer using a public key. A cryptographic hash can detect changes only when its expected digest is obtained through a trusted channel; an attacker can replace an unprotected file and its hash together.','MACs and digital signatures protect integrity.')
add(sec,'1(f)','Find Z*14 and phi(14)','Z*14 consists of residues from 0 to 13 having gcd(a,14)=1. Remove multiples of 2 and 7. The remaining residues are {1,3,5,9,11,13}. There are 6, so phi(14)=6. Also phi(14)=14(1-1/2)(1-1/7)=6.','Z*14={1,3,5,9,11,13}; phi(14)=6.')
add(sec,'1(g)','Why security by obscurity is insufficient','If security depends only on hiding the design, discovering or leaking that design defeats the defense. Publicly reviewed mechanisms can remain secure when the algorithm is known, provided keys remain secret. Obscurity may add friction but should not replace sound cryptography, access control and review.','Keep keys secret; do not depend solely on a secret design.')
add(sec,'1(h)','Why OFB cannot generate all blocks in parallel','OFB generates O_i=E_K(O_(i-1)), starting from an IV. Each keystream block therefore depends on its predecessor, so keystream generation is sequential. Once the keystream has been precomputed, XORing it with data blocks can be parallelized. CTR instead generates independent counter blocks.','OFB keystream generation is chained; only the later XOR stage is parallelizable.')
add(sec,'2(a)','Why asymmetric encryption is needed','Symmetric encryption is efficient but requires the parties to establish a shared secret securely. Public-key techniques help establish keys without first sharing that secret and support digital signatures. A hybrid system authenticates parties and establishes a session key using public-key mechanisms, then encrypts bulk data symmetrically. Public keys still need authentication to prevent substitution.','Public-key methods help key establishment and signatures; symmetric methods handle bulk data.')
add(sec,'2(b)','Security principles violated by the SUST scenario','A privately designed encrypted channel without demonstrated public scrutiny raises an open-design concern; merely being proprietary does not prove it is broken. Allowing every visitor to access every student result indicates missing authorization and least-privilege enforcement if those records are intended to be private. Encryption protects the channel but cannot correct overly broad application permissions. Use reviewed transport protection, authenticate users, authorize each record access and audit accesses. The scenario does not establish an availability failure.','Likely issues: open design, least privilege and confidentiality through missing authorization.')
add(sec,'2(c)','Compare Password A and Password B','A has 10 case-sensitive letters: 52^10=144,555,105,949,057,024 possibilities if sampled uniformly. B has 4 letters, 3 digits and 2 symbols, so it has 9 characters, not 10. Let s be the number of allowed symbols. If category positions are fixed, B has 52^4 x 10^3 x s^2 possibilities. If any arrangement is allowed, multiply by 9!/(4!3!2!)=1260. For exactly three allowed symbols, these counts are 65,804,544,000 and 82,913,725,440,000 respectively, both below A. The printed examples do not fix the symbol alphabet or position rule, so B has no single unqualified numerical count. Under these conventional assumptions and uniform selection A is stronger mathematically; practical strength depends on randomness, reuse, leaks and guessing patterns. Composition alone does not determine security.','A wins under the stated three-symbol examples; B requires explicit alphabet and placement assumptions.')
add(sec,'2(d)','AES mode for a video with missing blocks','CTR is a suitable confidentiality-mode answer: each block uses E_K(nonce,counter), so an independently addressed surviving block can be decrypted even when another is lost. CBC depends on neighboring ciphertext; OFB needs synchronized keystream position. ECB has independence but leaks patterns and should not be chosen for that reason. In a real streaming design, use authenticated encryption per independently numbered packet or chunk, with correct nonce uniqueness and replay handling. If the transport removes bytes without preserving offsets, no mode automatically restores synchronization.','CTR fits independent block access; practical systems should authenticate separate chunks.')
add(sec,'2(e)','Public-key versus private-key cryptography','Symmetric/private-key encryption uses the same secret key for encryption and decryption, is efficient for large data, and requires secure secret distribution. Asymmetric/public-key encryption uses a public/private key pair; data encrypted to a public key is decrypted with its private key. Public-key signatures use a signing key and a public verification key. They ease key distribution but require authentic public keys and are generally more expensive. Neither approach is inherently secure without good algorithms, key management and implementation.','Symmetric: shared secret and speed. Asymmetric: key pairs, key establishment and signatures.')
add(sec,'2(f)','Decrypt CB7F1 using the S-box','Read each input nibble as row=first two bits and column=last two bits. The table maps input 0..F to outputs 3,4,F,1,A,6,5,B,E,D,2,7,0,9,C. Correction: the full 16-entry sequence is 3,4,F,1,A,6,5,B,E,D,2,7,0,9,C only if an entry is omitted; use the explicitly reconstructed rows below instead.\nRows in the scan: 00 -> [3,4,F,1]; 01 -> [A,6,5,B]; 10 -> [E,D,2,7]; 11 -> [0,9,C] is not the actual scan. This transcription must be checked at full resolution before using a numerical result.','See the verified S-box transcription note.')
add(sec,'3(a)(i)','Design and verify an RSA e-certificate system','Setup: SUST creates an RSA signing key pair in protected storage and distributes an authenticated verification key through a trusted certificate chain. Issuance: form a canonical credential containing student identity, degree, issuer, unique serial and issue date; hash and sign it using a standard RSA signature scheme such as RSA-PSS. Holder: the student stores the document with its signature. Verification: the authority obtains the trusted issuer key, checks validity and revocation status, recomputes the credential hash and verifies the signature. A changed credential fails verification. In the textbook abstraction, s=m^d mod n and s^e mod n=m follows from the RSA exponent relation; real RSA-PSS verifies an encoded hash, not raw document exponentiation.','Issuer signs; student holds; verifier checks the signature using SUST’s authenticated public key.')
add(sec,'3(a)(ii)','Remaining threats to e-certificates','Consider theft of the issuer signing key, fraudulent issuance, forged identity at enrollment, malicious administrators, key substitution, implementation errors, altered verification software, replay of revoked credentials and denial of service. Use hardware-backed key protection, limited signing permissions, audited issuance, key rotation, revocation checking, secure verification and recovery procedures. A valid signature proves issuer authentication and integrity; it does not independently prove the issuer’s claim was truthful.','Protect issuance, signing keys, identity checks and revocation, not only the signature algorithm.')
add(sec,'3(b)(i)','Problems with AES-ECB for student data','ECB encrypts identical plaintext blocks to identical ciphertext blocks under the same key. It reveals repeated structures in images and documents and allows block substitution/reordering without built-in authentication. AES-256 key size does not remove these mode-level weaknesses. Use suitable authenticated encryption with carefully managed nonces.','ECB leaks patterns and provides no message authentication.')
add(sec,'3(b)(ii)','Would RSA be better for bulk student data?','RSA is unsuitable for directly encrypting large images and documents: inputs have strict size limits and operations are expensive. A hybrid design uses a fresh symmetric key for authenticated data encryption and protects that key with an appropriate public-key mechanism. RSA-OAEP is an example for key wrapping when the system requires RSA.','Use hybrid encryption, not direct RSA for entire documents.')
add(sec,'3(b)(iii)','Responding to a compromised encryption key','Contain the breach and stop new use of the key. Determine what data and systems were exposed; revoke or disable affected key access, generate a replacement and re-encrypt recoverable stored data under a sound design. Protect new key material, audit activity, restore trustworthy systems and follow applicable incident procedures. Rotation cannot make already copied plaintext or previously decrypted data secret again.','Contain, investigate, replace keys and re-encrypt; prior exposure cannot be undone.')
add(sec,'3(b)(iv)','Cloud-backup risks','Risks include misconfigured access, stolen credentials, compromised encryption keys, provider or insider access, deletion/ransomware, outages, loss of recoverability and jurisdictional obligations. Encrypt before upload where appropriate, protect keys separately, restrict permissions, enable versioning/immutability where available and test restores. An encrypted backup is useful only if authorized users can recover its key.','Secure permissions, keys, backup integrity and tested recovery.')
add(sec,'3(c)(i)','Why both password hashing and MACs?','Password storage needs a salted, deliberately expensive password-hashing scheme; verification repeats the calculation and compares the result. Transactions need authenticated integrity: a MAC computed with a secret key detects modification and authenticates a party holding that key. A transaction identifier or nonce is also needed to address replay. A plain fast hash does not securely replace a password hasher or a keyed MAC.','Password hashing protects stored verifiers; MACs authenticate transaction integrity.')
add(sec,'3(c)(ii)','Unsalted hashes and salting complexity','Without salts, equal passwords yield equal hashes and one precomputed dictionary can be compared with many accounts. Suppose there are U accounts, D guesses and cost C per hash. An attacker can compute D hashes, roughly D*C work, and look up matches across all unsalted accounts. Unique salts prevent reuse across accounts; attacking all accounts with D guesses each requires roughly U*D*C work. Attacking one chosen account still costs roughly D*C. A salt is stored openly and does not increase the password’s inherent entropy. Combine unique salts with a costly password-hashing scheme.','Salts defeat cross-account/precomputed reuse; they do not make one weak password unguessable.')
add(sec,'4(a)','Number of possible 1-inch QR patterns at 100 PPI','The idealized image contains 100 x 100=10,000 pixels. If every pixel independently has two colors, there are 2^10000 binary patterns. This is not the count of valid QR codes: real QR symbols use structured modules, finder patterns, encoding, version constraints, quiet zones and error correction. PPI alone cannot determine a valid-code count.','Ideal binary patterns: 2^10000. Valid QR-code count is not determined by PPI alone.')
add(sec,'4(b)','Primitive root','For a modulus n admitting primitive roots, g is a primitive root if its powers generate every element of the multiplicative group Z*n. Its multiplicative order is phi(n). For example, modulo 7 the powers of 3 yield 3,2,6,4,5,1, covering all nonzero residues. Not every modulus has a primitive root.','A generator of the multiplicative group modulo n.')
add(sec,'4(c)','Protocol for S to sign M and V to verify','S computes sigma=Sign(sk_S,M) and sends (M,sigma,issuer-certificate if required). V authenticates S’s public key, then checks Verify(pk_S,M,sigma). Accept the claimed signed message only when verification and relevant certificate/status checks succeed. A signature alone does not encrypt M, and freshness may require a nonce or timestamp.','S -> V: (M, signature); V verifies using S’s trusted public key.')
add(sec,'4(d)','Ransomware','Malware that denies access to data or systems, often by encryption, and demands payment; attackers may also steal data and threaten disclosure. Offline or immutable tested backups, least privilege, patching and incident containment reduce impact.','Malware that extorts payment by denying access or threatening data disclosure.')
add(sec,'4(e)','Interpret dr-xr---wx','Split the 10 characters as d | r-x | r-- | -wx. d means directory. Owner can list names and traverse/search, but cannot create/delete entries because write is absent. Group can list names but cannot normally traverse without execute permission. Others can write and traverse but cannot list names. Directory deletion permissions depend mainly on the containing directory and additional controls such as the sticky bit. Numeric permission bits are 543.','Directory; owner r-x, group r--, others -wx (543).')
add(sec,'4(f)','Firewall setting to avoid being a Smurf amplifier','Disable forwarding of directed broadcasts at network boundaries and prevent responding to ICMP echo requests addressed to broadcast destinations. Ingress/egress anti-spoofing also limits forged-source traffic. Simply disabling every kind of ICMP can impair legitimate network operation.','Block directed-broadcast amplification and enforce anti-spoofing.')
add(sec,'4(g)','Rainbow-table attack','An attacker uses precomputed hash/reduction chains to trade storage and precomputation for faster inversion of unsalted password hashes. Unique per-password salts make a single precomputed table impractical to reuse across accounts. Costly password hashing further increases guessing cost.','Precomputed chains accelerate guesses against unsalted password hashes.')
add(sec,'4(h)','Brute force and time-efficiency strategies','Brute force systematically tests candidate keys or passwords until a match is found or the search space is exhausted. Attackers can prioritize likely dictionary/mutation candidates, distribute independent guesses, use suitable hardware, or precompute against unsalted fast hashes. Prioritized guessing is a heuristic shortcut rather than exhaustive enumeration in a fixed order. Rate limiting and costly salted password hashing raise the cost; good randomness raises the search space.','Exhaustive candidate testing; prioritization, parallelism and reusable precomputation can reduce effort.')
add(sec,'5(a)','Virtual memory and efficient management','Virtual memory gives each process an address-space abstraction, isolation, protection and the ability to use more address space than physical RAM. The OS maps virtual pages to physical frames using page tables; the MMU translates addresses, and a TLB caches translations. Demand paging loads pages when needed. Good replacement policies, locality and sufficient memory limit expensive page faults. Virtual memory is not inherently faster than direct RAM access; TLBs and locality reduce its overhead.','Page tables map addresses; TLBs accelerate translation; paging must avoid excessive faults.')
add(sec,'5(b)','TCP session hijacking','At a conceptual level, an attacker attempts to impersonate a participant in an established TCP connection by obtaining enough session/sequence context to make injected traffic appear valid, or by compromising an endpoint or traffic path. TCP alone does not authenticate application data cryptographically. Authenticated TLS, secure endpoint handling, unpredictable initial sequence values and network anti-spoofing reduce risk; TLS is the core application-data defense.','Hijacking forges participation in an existing connection; authenticated encryption protects its data.')
add(sec,'5(c)','Toy Merkle-Damgard-style hash of THIEF','Use the printed recurrence d_i=(26*d_(i-1)+(M_i-\'A\'+1)) mod 107, with d_0=0 and A=1. T=20 gives d1=20. H=8 gives d2=(20*26+8) mod107=528 mod107=100. I=9 gives d3=2609 mod107=41. E=5 gives d4=1071 mod107=1. F=6 gives d5=32 mod107=32. Although the question mentions 8-bit quantities, its stated modulus is 107; follow that recurrence. The toy construction is not a secure general-purpose cryptographic hash.','Final hash = 32 decimal (0x20).')
add(sec,'5(d)','DNS name resolution','The application asks a resolver for a domain’s address. A cached answer may be returned immediately. Otherwise a recursive resolver typically queries a root server for the relevant top-level-domain referral, the TLD server for the authoritative server, and the authoritative server for the requested A/AAAA record, following aliases if necessary. It caches results according to TTL and returns them. This is name resolution; HTTPS separately authenticates the server reached.','Resolver -> root -> TLD -> authoritative answer, with caching and possible aliases.')
add(sec,'5(e)','Botnets and DDoS','A botnet is a collection of compromised devices remotely coordinated by an operator. A DDoS attack makes many devices generate traffic or resource-consuming requests against a target, exhausting network, compute or application capacity. Multiple sources complicate filtering. Layered protection includes traffic analysis, rate limits, upstream filtering, DDoS mitigation and resilient capacity planning.','Many compromised devices overwhelm one service together.')
add(sec,'5(f)','Trojans versus worms','A Trojan disguises malicious functionality as something legitimate and typically relies on a user installing or executing it; self-replication is not its defining trait. A worm self-propagates between systems, often by exploiting vulnerabilities. Malware can combine these behaviors.','Trojan: deceptive delivery. Worm: autonomous propagation.')
add(sec,'6(a)(i)','Can ElGamal distribute a key?','Yes: encode a suitable symmetric session key as a message in the supported group and encrypt it to the receiver’s authenticated public key. In practice use a vetted hybrid/key-encapsulation construction instead of ad hoc encoding. In this toy example the number M=43 can stand for key material; a modulus of 113 is far too small for real security. Textbook ElGamal is malleable and does not itself authenticate the sender.','ElGamal can carry key material in a hybrid design; authenticate keys and ciphertexts appropriately.')
add(sec,'6(a)(ii)','Compute the ElGamal ciphertext','Given p=113, g=34, Bob’s private x=97, Alice’s fresh k=37 and M=43. Bob publishes y=34^97 mod113=101. Alice computes c1=34^37 mod113=107 and shared factor s=101^37 mod113=45. Then c2=43*45 mod113=1935 mod113=14. One check for c1: 34^2=26, 34^4=111, 34^8=4, 34^16=16, 34^32=30 modulo113; 30*111*34 mod113=107. Ciphertext is the ordered pair (107,14).','Ciphertext (c1,c2) = (107,14).')
add(sec,'6(a)(iii)','Decrypt ElGamal and prove correctness','Bob calculates s=c1^x mod p=107^97 mod113=45. The inverse of 45 modulo113 is 108 because 45*108=4860=43*113+1. Therefore M=c2*s^(-1) mod113=14*108 mod113=1512 mod113=43. Generally c1^x=(g^k)^x=g^(kx)=(g^x)^k=y^k. Thus c2/(c1^x)=M*y^k/y^k=M in the multiplicative group.','14 x 108 mod113 = 43; the shared factors cancel.')
add(sec,'6(b)(i)','University portal redirected to a fake site','The scenario is consistent with DNS poisoning or another name-resolution compromise (pharming), so the correct hostname resolves to an attacker-controlled IP. The fake portal then performs phishing to collect credentials and card details. The symptoms alone do not prove the exact compromise: a hosts-file change, malicious resolver or registrar compromise could also explain them. A correctly validated HTTPS connection should reject an attacker lacking a valid certificate for the genuine hostname.','Likely pharming via compromised name resolution, followed by credential phishing.')
add(sec,'6(b)(ii)','Defenses against portal redirection','Protect registrar and DNS administration with strong authentication and least privilege; secure and patch resolvers; validate DNSSEC where deployed; use properly validated HTTPS and HSTS; protect endpoints; monitor DNS/certificate changes; and use phishing-resistant authentication where available. Notify affected users and revoke compromised sessions if an incident occurs. DNSSEC authenticates DNS data, while TLS authenticates the web endpoint; they address related but different layers.','Secure DNS administration and resolution, enforce HTTPS validation and strengthen authentication.')
add(sec,'6(c)(i)','How a TLS certificate helps against fake websites','During a correctly validated TLS handshake, the browser checks the certificate’s hostname, validity and chain to a trusted authority, and the server proves possession of the corresponding private key. An attacker serving a fake site at an unauthorized endpoint cannot normally impersonate the genuine hostname without satisfying these checks. The authenticated handshake establishes protected session traffic. A certificate identifies a domain/key binding; it does not certify that all business content is trustworthy.','Validated TLS authenticates the requested hostname and protects session traffic.')
add(sec,'6(c)(ii)','Why customers can still be victims','A phishing site may use a different, lookalike hostname with its own valid HTTPS certificate. Users can be misled by a padlock, bypass warnings, or use compromised devices. For example, a certificate for shop-example-login.com does not authenticate shop-example.com. HTTPS can protect the user’s connection to the wrong site.','A valid certificate for a lookalike domain does not make it the real shop.')
add(sec,'6(c)(iii)','Certificate and HTTPS best practices','Use a certificate from a trusted authority for the exact hostname, automate renewal, serve the full correct chain, protect private keys and revoke/reissue after compromise. Configure supported secure TLS, redirect HTTP to HTTPS, apply HSTS carefully, avoid mixed content and use secure session-cookie settings. Monitor expiry and certificate issuance. No configuration guarantees every browser will always trust a certificate; trust stores and client clocks also matter.','Maintain valid hostname certificates, safe TLS configuration, key protection and renewal.')
# Full-resolution visual verification of the printed substitution table.
item=next(i for i in sec['items'] if i['ref']=='2(f)')
item['body']='Read an input nibble as row=first two bits and column=last two bits. The printed rows, in hexadecimal, are 00: [3,4,F,1]; 01: [A,6,5,B]; 10: [E,D,4,2]; 11: [7,0,9,C]. Decryption searches for each ciphertext output and returns its input position. C occurs at input 1111=F; B at 0111=7; 7 at 1100=C; F at 0010=2; 1 at 0011=3. Therefore CB7F1 decrypts to F7C23. The scanned table repeats output 4 (at inputs 1 and A) and omits output 8, so it is not a bijection as printed. Those issues do not create ambiguity for this particular ciphertext, which contains neither 4 nor 8.'
item['final']='Plaintext F7C23; the printed S-box has a duplicate output elsewhere.'
hci=course('hci','HCI','Human–Computer Interaction','CSE 495',[5,6],'Design methods, usability, prototyping and user research, grounded in the exam scenarios.',['Double Diamond: Discover -> Define -> Develop -> Deliver.','UCD loop: understand context -> specify requirements -> design -> evaluate -> iterate.','Experiment: independent variables are manipulated; dependent variables are measured.','Keyboard experiment: 2 sizes x 3 layouts x 3 device configurations = 18 combinations.','Usability measures: task success, completion time, errors and satisfaction.'],['Yvonne Rogers, Helen Sharp and Jenny Preece, Interaction Design: Beyond Human-Computer Interaction.','Alan Dix, Janet Finlay, Gregory D. Abowd and Russell Beale, Human-Computer Interaction.','Jakob Nielsen, 10 Usability Heuristics: https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ten-usability-heuristics/'], 'The uploaded HCI header says 4th Year 2nd Semester (session 2020–21). It is included here as requested, without relabeling the original paper.')
add(hci,'1(a)','Why usability matters','Usability lets specified users achieve goals effectively, efficiently and with satisfaction in a specified context. It reduces errors, training effort and frustration, improving task completion and accessibility. Example: a clear course-registration flow helps students submit the right courses without repeated help.','Usability improves successful, efficient and satisfying task completion.')
add(hci,'1(b)','Universal Design principles','The seven commonly taught Universal Design principles are equitable use; flexibility in use; simple and intuitive use; perceptible information; tolerance for error; low physical effort; and size and space for approach and use. Example: a campus kiosk offers readable text, audio alternatives, reachable controls and confirmation before destructive actions. These are distinct from Nielsen’s usability heuristics.','Design for a broad range of abilities and contexts using the seven principles.')
add(hci,'1(c)','What is WIMP?','WIMP means Windows, Icons, Menus and Pointer. It describes a familiar graphical-interface style where users manage windows, recognize icons, choose menu commands and point to controls. Example: a desktop file manager.','Windows, Icons, Menus, Pointer.')
add(hci,'1(d)','What is the three-click rule?','It is a design heuristic claiming users should reach desired information within three clicks. Treat it as a historical guideline, not a validated universal limit: clear labels, meaningful progress and task success matter more than a fixed click count. A longer understandable path can be easier than three confusing clicks.','Keep navigation clear; three clicks is not a universal usability law.')
add(hci,'1(e)','Claims and assumptions in conceptual design','An assumption is a belief taken as true without yet having evidence, such as assuming all students own smartphones. A claim is an assertion about an expected design benefit, such as saying push reminders will reduce missed deadlines. Record both and test them through research or evaluation before relying on them.','Assumptions are untested beliefs; claims are assertions to investigate.')
add(hci,'1(f)','Mind maps','A mind map is a visual organization of ideas around a central topic. Branches show related concepts and sub-branches show detail. For a study app, branches might include courses, notes, revision and notifications. It supports brainstorming and seeing relationships rather than prescribing a rigid workflow.','A central idea with branching related concepts.')
add(hci,'1(g)','Problems with interface metaphors','A metaphor can imply incorrect behavior, transfer poorly across cultures, become confusing when pushed beyond its real-world meaning, or constrain better designs. A trash-can metaphor may confuse users about whether an item is recoverable. Use metaphors only where they clarify the system and support them with clear labels and feedback.','Metaphors help familiarity but can mislead or limit understanding.')
add(hci,'1(h)','Wizard-of-Oz prototyping','Users interact with what appears to be an automated system while a human secretly performs some system responses. A researcher might simulate a café chatbot before building its AI. It tests interaction ideas cheaply, but timing and behavior must be consistent, and research ethics/consent and debriefing should be handled appropriately.','A human simulates functionality behind an apparently working prototype.')
add(hci,'2(a)','Four stages of the Double Diamond','Discover: explore users and the problem broadly through interviews, observation and evidence. Define: synthesize findings into a clear problem statement. Develop: generate and prototype multiple solutions. Deliver: test, refine and implement a suitable solution. Each diamond expands possibilities and then narrows them. Example: investigate café queues, define pickup confusion, prototype alternatives, then test and launch an improved order flow.','Discover, Define, Develop, Deliver; diverge then converge twice.')
add(hci,'2(b)','Nine user-centered chapters','The wording appears to refer to a course-specific nine-step framework, but no lecture framework is attached. One defensible nine-stage study outline is: identify users and stakeholders; research context; understand goals and tasks; specify user requirements; generate concepts; structure information and flows; prototype; evaluate with users; iterate and monitor after release. Explain how each keeps user evidence central. Do not present this particular nine-step enumeration as a uniquely standardized UCD model; compare its labels with your lecture notes.','A nine-stage example is provided; the exact course-specific list needs the lecture source.')
add(hci,'2(c)','Slow, hard-to-navigate university portal','Slow responses harm efficiency and satisfaction; poor navigation harms learnability, findability and task success, and can increase errors. Measure completion time, success rate, navigation mistakes and user feedback. Improve information architecture, use consistent plain-language labels and visible navigation, show loading/progress feedback, optimize server and page performance, and test common tasks such as registration with students.','Improve performance and findability, then verify using usability measures.')
add(hci,'2(d)','Desktop, folders and trash can','This is an interface metaphor: familiar real-world concepts help users form a mental model of an unfamiliar digital system. A folder suggests grouping documents; a trash can suggests discarding items. This reduces initial learning effort, but the design should explain differences such as restoration and permanent deletion.','An interface metaphor transfers familiar concepts into a digital interface.')
add(hci,'2(e)','Organizing brainstorming sticky notes','Use affinity diagramming. Put each observation or idea on a separate note, silently group related notes, discuss and adjust the groups, label each cluster with a meaningful theme, and inspect relationships or higher-level themes. Preserve unusual notes rather than forcing everything into a category. Example clusters: navigation, payment, accessibility and feedback.','Affinity diagramming groups related ideas into labeled themes.')
add(hci,'3(a)','Low- and high-fidelity café app prototypes','A prototype is a representation used to explore and test a design before committing to a full implementation. Low fidelity: paper sketches of menu, cart, checkout and pickup; ask users to order while a facilitator switches screens. It is cheap, quick and good for comparing flows. High fidelity: a realistic clickable interface with representative menu data, order confirmation and simulated payment states; test labels, interaction details and error recovery. It costs more and can make unfinished features appear real. Compare what the evaluation needs to learn, then iterate; do not collect real card details in a simulated payment test.','Use low fidelity for early structure and high fidelity for realistic interaction testing.')
add(hci,'3(b)','Primary interaction types and examples','A commonly taught interaction-design set is instructing, conversing, manipulating and exploring. Instructing: choose Print or issue a command. Conversing: ask a voice assistant a question. Manipulating: drag a file or pinch a photo. Exploring: navigate a map or virtual environment. Some newer course frameworks also discuss responding to system prompts as an additional type; use your lecture’s taxonomy if it differs.','Instructing, conversing, manipulating and exploring, with course-dependent extensions.')
add(hci,'3(c)','UCD process for a mobile banking app','Understand the context: interview customers, including those with accessibility needs, about devices, connectivity and banking tasks. Specify requirements: safe transfers, clear balances, recovery and measurable usability goals. Produce design solutions: prototype account views, recipient selection, amount entry and confirmation. Evaluate with representative users using realistic tasks and safe test data. Iterate based on failures and evidence, then monitor after release. Security and usability requirements must be considered together.','Understand users -> specify requirements -> design -> evaluate -> iterate.')
add(hci,'4(a)','Ten-second rule in web design','The common guidance is that users should quickly understand a page’s purpose and value; after roughly ten seconds without engagement or useful feedback, attention may drift. It is a heuristic, not a hard universal time limit. Give a clear heading, relevant content and responsive progress feedback; do not imply that every task must finish in ten seconds.','Communicate purpose and progress quickly; ten seconds is a guideline.')
add(hci,'4(b)','Issues with long URLs','Long, opaque URLs are hard to remember, read, type and share, may wrap or be truncated, and can hide meaningful structure. Query strings can also accidentally expose sensitive information. Prefer stable, descriptive routes and avoid putting secrets in URLs. A long URL does not automatically make the underlying page unusable.','Long URLs reduce readability and shareability and can expose query data.')
add(hci,'4(c)','Think-aloud limitations','Speaking while working can slow participants, change their strategy and impose cognitive load. Some participants find it unnatural or cannot verbalize automatic behavior; retrospective explanations can be incomplete. Use neutral prompts, avoid coaching, and distinguish task-time measurements gathered under think-aloud from normal performance.','Verbalization can alter the behavior being measured.')
add(hci,'4(d)','Gathering subjective data','Use interviews, questionnaires, satisfaction scales, diaries and focus groups to collect perceptions and experiences. For example, a post-task ease rating measures perceived difficulty, while an interview explores why. Combine subjective reports with observed behavior; stated preference and actual success can differ.','Interviews, questionnaires, scales, diaries and focus groups capture user perceptions.')
add(hci,'4(e)','Why informed consent matters','Participants should understand the study’s purpose, procedures, expected risks, recording and data use, confidentiality arrangements and right to withdraw. Consent respects autonomy and supports ethical participation. Avoid coercion and collect only necessary data. Consent is a process, not merely a signature.','People must be able to make an informed, voluntary decision about participation.')
add(hci,'4(f)','Heuristic evaluation','Usability specialists inspect an interface against established principles such as visibility of status, consistency and error prevention. Evaluators first inspect independently, then consolidate findings and severity judgments. It is inexpensive early feedback but does not replace testing with representative users.','Expert inspection against usability principles.')
add(hci,'4(g)','Predictive evaluation methods','Predict performance without running a full user experiment using models such as GOMS or the Keystroke-Level Model. Fitts’s law can estimate pointing time from target distance and size, and Hick-Hyman-style models can describe choice-time effects under suitable assumptions. Predictions depend on model assumptions and should be checked against real tasks.','Model-based evaluation includes GOMS, KLM and suitable pointing/choice models.')
add(hci,'4(h)','People-centered approach','Design around people’s goals, capabilities, needs and real context, involving affected people throughout research and iteration. Consider diverse users and stakeholders rather than only technical convenience. Example: test a service with first-time users and people with disabilities, not just its developers.','Let evidence about people guide design and evaluation.')
add(hci,'5(a)','Smart speaker fails in background noise','This illustrates context-sensitive speech recognition and input ambiguity. Test in realistic acoustic conditions; improve noise handling and microphone placement; show or speak back recognized commands; ask for confirmation on consequential actions; allow correction and provide touch/text alternatives. Avoid repeatedly blaming the user for recognition failures.','Support noisy contexts with feedback, recovery and alternative inputs.')
add(hci,'5(b)','Mandatory account creation before checkout','A forced registration barrier adds unnecessary effort and limits user freedom, harming flexibility and efficiency. Offer guest checkout, request only essential information, explain why it is needed and allow optional account creation after purchase. Measure abandonment and task completion before and after changing the flow. There is no single universally named heuristic uniquely implied by this scenario.','Guest checkout reduces unnecessary effort and preserves user choice.')
add(hci,'5(c)','HCI principles for mobile and web','Use consistent navigation, readable hierarchy, clear status, error prevention/recovery and accessible controls. Small screens require prioritization, responsive layouts and progressive disclosure. Touch needs sufficiently large, separated targets and alternatives to hidden gestures; avoid hover-only access. Web layouts should support keyboard navigation, zoom, varied screen sizes and visible focus. Optimize loading and preserve task state across interruptions. Test on representative devices and assistive technologies.','Design for screen size, input method, accessibility, responsive layout and feedback.')
add(hci,'5(d)','Experiment: does a new UI reduce transaction time?','Define a hypothesis and primary outcome, such as successful transaction completion time. Independent variable: old versus new interface. Recruit representative users and assign conditions randomly in a between-subjects design, or counterbalance order in a within-subjects design. Keep tasks, device, network and instructions comparable. Pilot the procedure; record time, errors and success rather than ignoring failed tasks. Compare conditions with an appropriate analysis, report uncertainty/effect size and consider practical significance. Control practice and fatigue effects.','Manipulate interface version, control confounds and compare timed task performance.')
add(hci,'5(e)','Ethnographic study of older adults and smart homes','Observe participants in their normal homes over time, with informed consent and privacy protections. Use contextual observation, open interviews, field notes, participant diaries and device-interaction records where consented. Study routines, physical constraints, family support, workarounds, trust and breakdowns in context. A lab may miss shared-device practices or connectivity problems, while surveys may miss behavior participants take for granted. Analyze recurring patterns without treating all older adults as identical.','Study actual routines and workarounds in the home through observation and contextual inquiry.')
add(hci,'6(a)','Five steps of usability testing for e-commerce','1. Plan: define target users, research questions, metrics and representative tasks such as finding and buying an item. 2. Recruit: select representative participants and arrange accessible, ethical participation. 3. Prepare: create a realistic test environment, safe test accounts, tasks and a pilot. 4. Conduct: give neutral tasks, observe without coaching, and record success, time, errors and comments. 5. Analyze and improve: group problems, prioritize by severity/frequency, revise and retest. This is a practical five-step organization; textbooks may divide these activities differently.','Plan, recruit, prepare, conduct, analyze and improve.')
add(hci,'6(b)','Hypotheses and keyboard experiment variables','A hypothesis is a testable statement about a population or effect. H0 usually expresses no difference/effect; H1 expresses a difference, or a specified direction if defined in advance. Here H0 states no performance or preference differences across the keyboard conditions. Independent variables: keyboard size (small, large: 2 levels); layout (alphabetic, QWERTY, random: 3); device/input configuration (touch large screen, mouse monitor, stylus PDA: 3). A full crossing gives 2*3*3=18 conditions. Dependent variables: completion/typing time, error rate and preference on a 5-point Likert scale. Control variables might include text length/difficulty, instructions and environment. Counterbalance order to limit learning/fatigue. Because device and input method are bundled, their separate causal effects cannot be isolated from this design alone.','Three factors with 2,3,3 levels; measure time, errors and preference; control other influences.')
add(hci,'6(c)','Focus groups and effective interviews','A moderator gathers a small, relevant group to discuss experiences using an open discussion guide. Obtain consent, encourage quieter participants, prevent domination, probe disagreements and record/analyze themes. Use findings to refine interpretations, then triangulate with observation or individual interviews; group agreement alone is not proof. Effective interviews use neutral open questions, ask for concrete recent examples, probe gently, listen actively, avoid leading or double-barreled questions and summarize interpretations for clarification.','Moderate balanced group discussion and use neutral, evidence-seeking interview questions.')
spm=course('spm','SPM','Software Project Management','CSE 421',[7,8],'Planning, estimation, quality and risk, including the exact COCOMO constants printed in the paper.',['PDCA: Plan -> Do -> Check -> Act.','SMART: Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Relevant, Time-bound (wording varies by course).','Basic COCOMO: effort E = a x KLOC^b person-months.','Schedule T = c x E^d months. Average staff = E/T.','Use the supplied exam constants: Organic (2,1,2,1), Semi-detached (3,2,2,1), Embedded (4,3,2,1).','W5HH: Why, What, When, Who, Where, How, How much.','Risk exposure is often modeled as probability x impact, using compatible units.'],['Bob Hughes and Mike Cotterell, Software Project Management.','Roger S. Pressman and Bruce R. Maxim, Software Engineering: A Practitioner’s Approach.','Barry W. Boehm, Software Engineering Economics.','Steve McConnell, Rapid Development.','ISO 9001:2015 (historical exam context): https://www.iso.org/standard/62085.html'], 'The final paper totals 50 marks over 2 hours. Its COCOMO constants differ from common textbook tables; the worked answer uses the printed constants.')
add(spm,'1(a)','Internal stakeholders','Internal stakeholders belong to the organization undertaking the project and affect or are affected by it. Examples include the project sponsor, project manager, developers, testers, operations staff and department managers. Distinguish them from external clients, regulators and suppliers.','Stakeholders inside the developing organization, such as the manager and development team.')
add(spm,'1(b)','Generic software','Generic software is developed for a broader market rather than one specified customer. The supplier typically controls product requirements and sells or distributes it to many users. Examples include a word processor, a general accounting package or an off-the-shelf photo editor. Bespoke software is commissioned for a particular customer’s needs.','A product made for many customers rather than one bespoke client.')
add(spm,'1(c)','Code-Like-Hell Programming','This classical mistake means rushing into intensive coding without adequate requirements, design, planning or disciplined control. It can appear productive initially but creates integration failures, rework and unpredictable delivery. Avoid it with proportionate planning, clear priorities, incremental delivery and engineering discipline.','Unplanned frantic coding creates rework and schedule risk.')
add(spm,'1(d)','Short-Changed Upstream Activities','This mistake underinvests in early work such as feasibility, requirements, architecture and planning to appear to save time. Ambiguities and design weaknesses then become expensive downstream defects. Invest enough in early clarification and review without demanding perfect upfront knowledge.','Skipping essential early analysis shifts greater cost into later rework.')
add(spm,'1(e)','Project scope','Scope defines the work and deliverables included in a project, together with boundaries, exclusions and acceptance conditions. Example: a library system includes catalog search and lending, but excludes payroll. A clear scope supports estimates and controls scope creep through an agreed change process.','Scope states what the project will and will not deliver.')
add(spm,'1(f)','PDCA loop with a software example','Plan: identify a problem and define a measurable improvement, such as reducing deployment failures. Do: pilot automated checks in one team. Check: compare failure data with the baseline. Act: standardize the successful change or revise the approach and repeat. PDCA is an iterative improvement loop, not a one-time checklist.','Plan an improvement, try it, assess evidence and adopt or revise it.')
add(spm,'1(g)','SMART objectives','A common expansion is Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Relevant and Time-bound. Example: reduce the median checkout response time below two seconds under a defined test load by the end of the next release. Some course texts use Assignable or Realistic in place of particular words; use the lecture’s variant if supplied.','Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Relevant, Time-bound.')
add(spm,'2(a)','Software crisis: reasons and example','The software crisis describes persistent difficulty delivering large software systems on time, within budget and with acceptable quality. Causes include growing complexity, unclear or changing requirements, weak estimates, poor communication, inadequate methods and insufficient testing. Example: a payroll project estimated as simple expands to complex tax and integration requirements, causing delays and incorrect payments. This is an illustrative example, not a claim about a named historical project.','Complexity and weak requirements, planning and engineering cause cost, schedule and quality failures.')
add(spm,'2(b)','Five project-management process groups','Initiating: authorize a project and identify stakeholders, such as approving a student portal charter. Planning: define scope, schedule, resources, quality and risk plans. Executing: coordinate people to build and test deliverables. Monitoring and controlling: compare actual progress with the plan and manage changes, defects and risks. Closing: obtain acceptance, hand over operations, close contracts and record lessons. These groups can recur or overlap; they are not necessarily five sequential SDLC phases.','Initiate, plan, execute, monitor/control and close.')
add(spm,'2(c)','CI/CD pipeline','Continuous integration frequently merges small changes into a shared repository, triggering automated build and test checks. Continuous delivery keeps tested changes deployable, often with a manual production approval. Continuous deployment automatically releases changes that pass the defined gates. Example pipeline: commit -> build -> unit/integration/security checks -> staging -> acceptance gates -> release -> monitoring. Failed checks block promotion and provide feedback.','Automate integration, verification and safe release; delivery and deployment differ in production approval.')
add(spm,'2(d)','Risk versus opportunity','A risk is an uncertain event or condition that can affect objectives; many project frameworks include both threats and opportunities. In common classroom contrast, risk means a downside threat and opportunity means a beneficial uncertainty. Real-life threat: rain may delay an event; opportunity: unexpectedly good weather may increase turnout. Software threat: a dependency may fail; opportunity: a reusable component may shorten delivery. Manage threats and opportunities deliberately rather than assuming uncertainty is always negative.','Threats can harm objectives; opportunities can improve them; both involve uncertainty.')
add(spm,'2(e)','Person-month, Brooks’s law and cost drivers','A person-month measures effort equivalent to one person working for one month under a stated convention; it is not the same as elapsed duration. Brooks’s law warns that adding people to a late software project can make it later because onboarding and communication consume time and some work cannot be parallelized. COCOMO cost drivers are factors such as required reliability, complexity, team capability and tool support that adjust effort in intermediate/detailed models. Basic COCOMO uses size and mode coefficients; do not silently add an unspecified effort multiplier.','Effort differs from duration; added staff have overhead; cost drivers adjust model effort.')
add(spm,'2(f)','Manage development of a YouTube-like platform','Initiate: establish the audience, business goal and feasibility. Plan: define an MVP such as accounts, upload, playback and search; estimate effort/cost, build a WBS, schedule milestones and identify scalability, moderation and privacy risks. Execute: build incrementally with architecture, implementation, automated tests and deployment work. Monitor/control: track cost, schedule, playback performance, defect rates and scope changes; respond to risks. Close/handover: obtain acceptance, document operations and recovery, train support and record lessons. Treat future features as planned increments rather than unlimited initial scope.','Define and estimate an MVP, deliver iteratively, monitor quality/risk and hand over operations.')
add(spm,'3(a)','W5HH principle with examples','Why is the system being developed? To reduce payroll errors. What will be done? Employee records and salary processing. When will it be done? Agreed milestone dates. Who is responsible? Named owners for requirements, implementation and testing. Where are they organizationally located? Finance, engineering and operations roles. How will the job be done? Chosen process, architecture and tools. How much resource is needed? Estimated effort, staffing and budget. These seven questions align purpose, responsibility and execution.','Why, What, When, Who, Where, How and How much.')
add(spm,'3(b)','COCOMO: payroll versus flight control','Use Basic COCOMO E=a*KLOC^b, T=c*E^d and average people=E/T. Classify the small simple payroll as Organic and the complex real-time hardware-dependent flight-control system as Embedded. Both use KLOC=40.\nPayroll: printed Organic constants a=2,b=1,c=2,d=1. E=2*40^1=80 person-months. T=2*80^1=160 months. Average staff=80/160=0.5 people.\nFlight control: printed Embedded constants a=4,b=3,c=2,d=1. E=4*40^3=4*64,000=256,000 person-months. T=2*256,000=512,000 months. Average staff=256,000/512,000=0.5 people.\nFlight-control effort and duration are each 3,200 times payroll under this supplied table. These extreme durations and fractional average staffing expose the unusual constants; they are algebraic model outputs, not credible staffing recommendations. Do not replace the paper’s coefficients with familiar textbook values without saying so. Fractional average effort can mean part-time allocation; it is not a literal half person.','Using the printed constants: payroll E=80, T=160; flight E=256000, T=512000; both E/T=0.5.')
add(spm,'3(c)','Risk in the Slack surge scenario','Treat the scenario as the exam’s hypothetical account, not a verified incident history. The direct issue is a technical/operational capacity and availability risk: demand exceeds the service’s ability to operate reliably. It can be called a performance or scalability risk depending on the course taxonomy. Reduce it with realistic load/stress testing, capacity forecasting, scaling plans, queueing and graceful degradation, monitoring, redundancy and an incident/recovery plan. Contingency capacity prepares for spikes rather than merely responding after failure.','A capacity/scalability and availability risk, addressed through testing, resilient design and planning.')
add(spm,'4(a)','Continuous Process Improvement','CPI is ongoing, evidence-based refinement of how work is performed to improve quality, efficiency or reliability. Measure a baseline, identify a bottleneck, try a change and inspect results. Example: use defect data to improve reviews, then verify whether escaped defects decrease.','Repeated measurable improvement of the way work is done.')
add(spm,'4(b)','MoSCoW prioritization','Must have: essential for a viable release, such as correct payment calculation. Should have: important but can temporarily be worked around, such as exportable reports. Could have: desirable if capacity permits, such as theme customization. Won’t have this time: explicitly excluded from the current scope, such as advanced forecasting. Agree criteria and prevent every feature being labeled Must.','Must, Should, Could, Won’t have this time.')
add(spm,'4(c)','Two reasons for risk management','First, it identifies threats early so the team can reduce their likelihood or impact before they cause major rework. Second, it prepares contingency actions and resource decisions, improving the predictability of cost, schedule and quality. It does not eliminate every uncertainty.','Reduce preventable losses and prepare responses to uncertainty.')
add(spm,'4(d)','Blended approach to risk management','A reasonable interpretation combines proactive prevention with reactive contingency and recovery. For data loss, improve storage reliability and access controls proactively, while retaining tested backups and an incident response plan for failures. The term can be course-specific; if your lecture defines a particular blend or framework, use its definition.','Combine prevention with prepared response and recovery; check course-specific terminology.')
add(spm,'4(e)','Purpose of Jenkins','Jenkins is an automation server commonly used to run CI/CD pipelines. It can trigger builds and tests after commits, coordinate checks and automate deployment steps through configured jobs and plugins. It supports the workflow; it does not itself guarantee software quality.','Automate builds, tests and delivery workflows.')
add(spm,'4(f)','Software Process Improvement','SPI systematically evaluates and changes software development/maintenance processes to improve outcomes such as product quality, delivery predictability and efficiency. Examples include adding peer review or improving defect tracking and checking whether results improve. Frameworks may guide assessment, but outcomes matter more than documentation alone.','Improve software processes through assessment, change and measurement.')
add(spm,'4(g)','Risk mitigation','Mitigation takes actions to lower a threat’s probability and/or impact. Example: automated regression tests reduce the chance of release defects, and rollback capability reduces their operational impact. Strict taxonomies distinguish mitigation from avoidance, transfer and acceptance; some exam wording uses mitigation broadly for all responses.','Reduce the probability or impact of an identified threat.')
add(spm,'5(a)','Key clauses of ISO 9001','For the ISO 9001:2015 structure commonly taught with this exam, clauses are: 1 Scope; 2 Normative references; 3 Terms and definitions; 4 Context of the organization; 5 Leadership; 6 Planning; 7 Support; 8 Operation; 9 Performance evaluation; 10 Improvement. Clauses 4-10 contain the main quality-management requirements. The question does not specify an edition, so identify the 2015 assumption; standards can change and this is a historical exam answer rather than a claim about the latest edition.','For ISO 9001:2015, main requirement clauses are Context, Leadership, Planning, Support, Operation, Evaluation and Improvement.')
add(spm,'5(b)','Why WBS matters for scope','A work breakdown structure decomposes the full project scope into manageable deliverables and work packages. It reveals omissions, supports estimates and responsibility assignment, and provides a baseline for change control. Apply the 100% rule: child elements collectively cover the parent scope without double-counting. A WBS is not by itself a timeline or dependency network.','WBS makes scope complete, estimable and assignable.')
add(spm,'5(c)','Project manager’s role in planning and scope','The manager coordinates stakeholders to clarify goals, requirements, exclusions and acceptance criteria; creates or coordinates the scope baseline and WBS; estimates resources, cost and schedule; assigns responsibilities; and establishes risk, communication and change-control plans. Example: for a portal, agree that registration is included but hostel allocation is deferred, then assess any request to add it against time and cost.','Align expectations, establish the plan and control scope changes.')
add(spm,'5(d)','Proactive versus reactive risk strategies','Proactive management identifies and analyzes possible problems before they occur and implements preventive measures, such as load-testing before registration day. Reactive management responds after a problem occurs, such as restoring service after an outage. Prepared contingency responses are planned proactively but executed when triggered. Good management combines prevention with recovery readiness.','Proactive reduces risks beforehand; reactive addresses their realized consequences.')
add(spm,'5(e)','Software versus traditional project management','Both manage scope, time, cost, people, quality and risk. Software adds distinctive challenges: intangible deliverables, high requirements volatility, complex integration, difficult progress visibility, knowledge-intensive work and cheap replication after development. Iterative delivery and automated tests can give early feedback. Avoid claiming traditional projects never change or never use iterative methods; the differences are matters of degree and domain.','Shared management foundations, with greater software-specific uncertainty and intangible progress.')
add(spm,'5(f)','Major causes of software project failure','Common contributors include unclear goals, unstable/uncontrolled scope, unrealistic estimates, weak stakeholder engagement, poor communication, missing skills/resources, architectural/integration problems, inadequate testing and unmanaged risk. These reinforce one another: rushed planning creates an impossible schedule, which encourages skipped checks and rework. Define failure against agreed goals rather than assuming delay is the only criterion.','Requirements, planning, communication, engineering quality and risk failures often interact.')
add(spm,'6(a)','WBS for an e-commerce website','Level 1: complete e-commerce website. Level 2: major deliverables such as project management, storefront, account/identity, catalog/search, cart/checkout, order management, platform/integration, quality/security and release/handover. Level 3: decompose checkout into address entry, delivery choice, payment integration and confirmation. Level 4: create estimable work packages with owner, acceptance criteria and boundaries, such as implementing and testing the payment callback. Include cross-cutting work explicitly, cover 100% of the agreed scope and avoid duplicated work. Add dependencies and dates in a schedule after defining the WBS.','Decompose the whole product into deliverables, components and manageable work packages.')
add(spm,'6(b)','CMM level and all higher maturity levels','Using the classic Software CMM taxonomy, organization-wide standardized and documented SDLC processes plus training point to Level 3: Defined. At Level 3, processes are established and tailored consistently across projects. Level 4: Managed adds quantitative measurement and statistical control of process/product performance. Level 5: Optimizing emphasizes continuous process improvement, defect prevention and innovation using quantitative evidence. Context: Level 1 is Initial and Level 2 is Repeatable. Do not silently mix these names with CMMI, whose level 2 and level 4 labels differ. The scenario suggests a level; a formal appraisal would need more evidence.','Likely CMM Level 3 Defined; then Level 4 Managed and Level 5 Optimizing.')
add(spm,'6(c)','Four risk-response strategies for the data-loss scenario','Avoid: remove the risky activity, for example do not store unnecessary sensitive customer data. Reduce/mitigate: use reliable storage to lower likelihood, and tested daily backups to lower impact; these are two dimensions of the same response category. Transfer/share: buy cyber-insurance for specified financial consequences; it does not remove technical responsibility. Accept: knowingly tolerate small residual corruption risks with monitoring and a defined response. The question asks for four strategies, but its explicit reliability and backup examples both represent reduction. The avoidance example is added to complete the standard four-strategy set.','Avoid, reduce, transfer and accept; reliability and backups both belong to reduction.')
tt=course('spm-term','SPM term test','SPM · Term Test','CSE 421',[9],'A separate term-test collection, including the original instructions and an illustrative response.',['20 minutes; 20 marks as printed.','Task 1: create 3 questions (4 marks each), answer any 1. Printed award: 6+4 marks.','Task 2: create 2 questions (4 marks each), answer any 1. Printed award: 4+2 marks.','Task 3: create 2 questions (2 marks each), answer both. Printed award: 2+2 marks.','Questions must not be identical to or closely resemble the final-exam questions.'],spm['refs'][:3], 'This is a question-setting exercise, not a conventional fixed question paper. All seven sample questions below are answered for study. The printed question-values and answer-award values are inconsistent in Task 2; the original scan is retained.')
add(tt,'Instructions','Original term-test tasks and marking','Time: 20 minutes. Total: 20 marks. Task 1 asks you to create three 4-mark questions about CPI, SPI and frameworks, then answer any one; awards are shown as 3*2=6 for creation and 4 for the answer. Task 2 asks you to create two 4-mark questions about planning, scope and WBS, then answer one; awards shown are 2*2=4 and 2 for the answer, despite the earlier 4-mark wording. Task 3 asks for two 2-mark questions about COCOMO’s concept and importance, then answers to both; awards shown are 2*1=2 and 2*1=2. The note disallows questions identical to or closely resembling the final exam. The seven examples here use different tasks and contexts, but the instructor decides whether they are sufficiently distinct.','Prepare original questions; the examples below answer every generated question, not just the required selections.')
add(tt,'1.1','Sample 4-mark question: assess whether a review pilot worked','Question: A team pilots peer review on one module and sees fewer escaped defects. What evidence is needed before extending the change across the organization? Explain how to avoid a misleading improvement claim.\nAnswer: Define the baseline, measurement period and escaped-defect rate relative to comparable size or usage. Check whether the module, team experience, release size or test effort changed at the same time. Record review effort and cycle time as well as defects, so improvement in one metric does not hide excessive cost. Replicate or extend the pilot cautiously, collect team feedback and decide whether to standardize or revise the change. Use a repeatable improvement cycle and preserve the evidence.','Compare normalized baseline and pilot outcomes, inspect confounds and repeat before standardizing.')
add(tt,'1.2','Sample 4-mark question: choose process measures','Question: A company reports 100% compliance with its new development checklist, yet customer defects keep rising. Propose four measures that could reveal whether its process improvement is useful.\nAnswer: Track escaped defects per release or unit of delivered functionality; track lead time from approved work to release; track change failure/recovery outcomes; and collect user-reported task problems or satisfaction for the changed product. Define each consistently and compare over a suitable period. Inspect changes in release complexity and reporting behavior. Checklist compliance is an activity measure, while these outcomes test whether the process achieves its goals.','Measure quality, delivery flow, release reliability and user outcomes, not compliance alone.')
add(tt,'1.3','Sample 4-mark question: align improvement frameworks','Question: Two teams use different improvement frameworks and want to share lessons without forcing identical workflows. Design a lightweight coordination approach.\nAnswer: Agree common business outcomes and a small measurement vocabulary while allowing each team to tailor its workflow. Share an improvement backlog, pilot hypotheses, baseline/results and lessons in periodic reviews. Map comparable practices across frameworks rather than equating certification labels. Adopt only changes supported by evidence and revisit them when context changes. This combines shared learning with local adaptation.','Share outcomes, evidence and learning while tailoring team practices.')
add(tt,'2.1','Sample 4-mark question: repair overlapping work packages','Question: An online booking project assigns both the payment team and notification team responsibility for sending payment receipts. How would you repair the WBS and acceptance criteria?\nAnswer: Identify one accountable work-package owner for the receipt capability and document the interface: payment publishes a confirmed-payment event; notification consumes it and sends the receipt. Define behavior for duplicates, failures and retries, with explicit tests and ownership. Remove duplicate scope/cost entries, ensure every necessary activity still belongs somewhere and update the WBS dictionary and estimates. Dependency timing belongs in the schedule.','Clarify boundaries, ownership and interfaces; remove double-counted scope.')
add(tt,'2.2','Sample 4-mark question: evaluate a late accessibility request','Question: A library-booking project receives a request for screen-reader support shortly before release. What planning evidence should be examined before agreeing a revised delivery commitment?\nAnswer: Determine whether accessibility was already a requirement or acceptance obligation; an omitted requirement is not automatically optional new scope. Identify affected pages, interaction and testing work, dependencies, specialist support and user impact. Compare delivery options, estimates, risk and resources, including phased delivery only where acceptable. Record the agreed baseline/change decision and communicate revised acceptance criteria and dates to stakeholders.','Check existing obligations, affected work, evidence-based estimates and an explicit change decision.')
add(tt,'3.1','Sample 2-mark question: compare COCOMO size revisions','Question: With unchanged Basic COCOMO mode coefficients, an estimate grows from 20 KLOC to 30 KLOC. Express the effort ratio without choosing numerical coefficients.\nAnswer: E1=a*20^b and E2=a*30^b. Divide to cancel a: E2/E1=(30/20)^b=1.5^b. Thus the effect depends on the size exponent; it is not necessarily a 50% effort increase.','Effort ratio = 1.5^b.')
add(tt,'3.2','Sample 2-mark question: sensitivity and estimation confidence','Question: Why should a manager present a COCOMO estimate as a range when requirements and size are uncertain? State two reasons.\nAnswer: First, effort depends on uncertain size, so plausible size bounds produce different effort outputs and can be amplified by the exponent. Second, calibration assumptions about the project/team may be imperfect, so one precise-looking output can hide model error. Present scenarios and update them as evidence improves.','Size uncertainty and model/calibration uncertainty justify a range.')
